      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/20-sticklebacks/gasterosteus-aculeatus-three-spined-stickleback/",
      "html": "<p>The Three-spined Stickleback (Gasterosteus aculeatus) is the most interesting and beautiful native aquarium fish. Its behavior has been intensively studied scientifically and the courtship, nesting and mating behavior of the species are still an integral part of school lessons today. The stickleback is a model organism for instinct research.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/20-sticklebacks/gasterosteus-aculeatus-three-spined-stickleback/",
      "html": "<p>The animals come from German fish farms. Unfortunately, nowadays most students and adults know these animals only from movies and books.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/20-sticklebacks/gasterosteus-aculeatus-three-spined-stickleback/",
      "html": "<p>Three-spined sticklebacks live in practically all of Europe, large parts of northern Asia and North America.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/20-sticklebacks/gasterosteus-aculeatus-three-spined-stickleback/",
      "html": "<p>Systematically-scientifically, the three-spined stickleback remains poorly understood. Over the centuries, 47 species of threespine stickleback have been described, but only a few of them are valid, the vast majority are seen as synonyms. In terms of behavior, a distinction is made between a stationary freshwater form and a migratory form that spends large portions of the year in the sea and returns to freshwater only to breed; in addition, there are apparently purely marine populations. Anatomically, there have been repeated attempts to use the number of lateral body plates as an identifier for species. For example, Gasterosteus gymnurus was for a long time considered a valid species lacking these plates on the caudal peduncle. However, recent DNA-based studies could not find any differences between G. gymnurus and G. aculeatus.</p>"
    }
  ],
